Its derivative at zero is the direct sum of the original two tangent inclusions, hence is invertible by transversality. After restricting its original coordinate neighbourhood it is a diffeomorphism. Moreover

Their interiors, where \(x,y>0\), avoid both sheets by \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.2} and the injectivity of the charts. Shrink the two disjoint endpoint neighbourhoods so they avoid every other sphere.

\subsubsection{Strips joining the two quadrants}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7.2}

Along the interior of \(\alpha\), choose a smooth vector field \(\eta_A\) transverse to \(TA\). Near \(p\) it must equal the \(y\)-coordinate field of \(\mathcal H_p\), and near \(q\) the \(y\)-coordinate field of \(\mathcal H_q\). These prescribed endpoint fields are transverse. The choices between them exist because the quotient \(TN'|_\alpha/TA|_\alpha\) is a rank-three bundle over an interval. A frame of that bundle is obtained by the projector transport \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:6.3} along the interval. In that frame the two endpoint nonzero vectors can be joined through \(\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\}\), for example by two straight segments through a third vector not on either forbidden ray, followed by smoothing with the endpoints fixed. Interpolate the remaining tangent components independently. Taking the interpolation constant near each prescribed endpoint neighbourhood preserves every already specified derivative.

Along \(\beta\), choose \(\eta_B\) transverse to \(TB\), equal to the \(x\)-coordinate fields in the endpoint charts. The same argument now uses the connected space \(\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}\). A path through it can be written by a continuous angle and positive radius along the interval; interpolation of the angle and positive radius, constant near the prescribed endpoint pieces, gives the required smooth path with no zero.

Extend each vector field to an ambient neighbourhood, using local coordinate extensions and a partition of unity equal to the original field along the arc. Make the extensions exactly the respective coordinate fields near the endpoints. Their local flows define the two strips

\begin{equation}
S_A(s,t)=\operatorname{Fl}^{\,t}_{\eta_A}(\alpha(s)),\qquad
S_B(s,t)=\operatorname{Fl}^{\,t}_{\eta_B}(\beta(s)),\qquad t\geq0.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.4}
\end{equation}

Near each endpoint these formulas agree exactly with \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.3}, by uniqueness of the flow of the coordinate field. At \(t=0\), each strip derivative has rank two. Compactness gives a uniform positive width on which they are embeddings: a sequence of new double points with widths tending to zero would limit either to two distinct points of the embedded original arc, or to the same point where a local embedding chart excludes them. The same argument keeps the strips apart away from their prescribed overlapping endpoint quadrants.

For sufficiently small width, the interior of the \(A\)-strip is disjoint from \(A\). Indeed in a local defining map for \(A\), its derivative in the \(t\)-direction is the nonzero normal component of \(\eta_A\). The expansion is \(t\nu(s)+O(t^2)\), with a uniform positive lower bound for \(\|\nu(s)\|\) on each compact part away from the endpoint charts; hence it cannot vanish for small positive \(t\). The endpoint charts already prove this there. The same argument applies to the \(B\)-strip and \(B\). Each strip away from the endpoints has a positive compact separation from every sphere its original arc avoids. Reducing its width retains all those separations. Thus the union of the quadrants and strips is an embedded collar of the two-arc circle, with two outer corners, and its interior avoids every \(A_\ell,B_k\).

\subsubsection{Filling without intersecting the retained collar}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:whitney-filled-collar}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7.3}

By \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:3.5}, the full complement \(N'\setminus\bigcup_kB_k\) has the same fundamental group as \(N\), hence is simply connected. The inner curve \(\gamma\) lies in this complement by the explicit collar construction. It therefore bounds a continuous disk there.

Extend \(C\) a little across its smooth inner boundary by the transverse-flow collar extension used in \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:5.14}. Take this extension on the side away from \(C\). It is disjoint from \(C\) except on \(\gamma\), and is disjoint from all the sphere systems for sufficiently small width. Parametrize a closed subcollar of this extension as the prescribed boundary collar of an actual round disk \(D_h^2\), with \(h>0\) retained. Its outer boundary parametrizes \(\gamma\); its interior lies on the new inward side.

Apply \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-5} with

\begin{equation}
K=\bigcup_k B_k,\qquad
\text{additional surfaces } A_1,\ldots,A_s,C.
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.8}
\end{equation}

The last additional surface is exactly the original compact annulus with its outer corners. Its use is justified by the boundary-and-corner strata proved after \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:5.13}. The prescribed disk collar interior avoids all these additional surfaces. The loop bounds a disk in \(N'\setminus K\), so all the hypotheses of the construction following \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:5.14} hold. We obtain a smooth embedded filling \(F:D_h^2\to N'\), whose interior avoids \(C\), every \(A_\ell\), and every \(B_k\), and which agrees with the entire prescribed inward collar.

Glue this filling to the unchanged \(C\) along \(\gamma\). Their surface charts and derivatives coincide there because both use the same extended collar. Their interiors are disjoint by \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.8}. Consequently their union \(D\) is an embedded disk with exactly the two original boundary corners. More explicitly, its domain is the annulus domain just described glued to \(D_h^2\) by the original boundary parametrization; it is topologically a disk, and its smooth structure consists of the original quadrant, strip, and filling charts. The map to \(N'\) is injective by the proved avoidance, is an immersion in each of those charts, and is an embedding by compactness. This construction does not require identifying a smooth round boundary with a corner boundary.

\subsubsection{An adapted frame on the complete annulus}
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:whitney-adapted-frame}

\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section-7.5}

Along the \(A\)-arc, let \(e_1\) be the normal projection of its chosen extra tangent vector. It never vanishes: the disk tangent plane meets \(TA\) exactly in the arc tangent line, by the transverse strip construction. Positive completions \((e_1,e_2,e_3)\) form a connected space. Indeed split the three-dimensional normal space into the first line and its metric orthogonal two-plane, only for this calculation. The projected pair of the last two vectors is a positive basis of that two-plane, its matrix belongs to \(GL^+(2,\mathbb R)\), and its two components along the first line are arbitrary. The positive basis space is connected by the full two-dimensional version of \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:6.17}--\eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:6.20}: retain its positive upper-triangular factor and join its rotation by an angle path. The two arbitrary line components can be interpolated directly. Thus along the interval we can choose a smooth completion agreeing with the exact corner frame on neighbourhoods of both endpoints. We keep these full nonorthogonal vectors, not only their orthonormal comparisons.

Along the \(B\)-arc, choose a smoothly varying pair \(v,w\) transverse to its tangent in \(TB\) and agreeing with the corner fields near its ends. Its two normal projections \(e_2,e_3\) are independent, since the disk plane meets \(TB\) exactly in the arc tangent line. Vectors \(e_1\) giving the positive triple \((e_1,e_2,e_3)\) form an open half-space. Interpolation in a frame along the interval therefore joins the prescribed corner first vectors with no determinant zero. The sign agreement \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.10} is exactly what puts both endpoint choices in this same half-space. This gives an adapted frame along both boundary arcs, equal to \(e_C\) near the corners.

Here is an explicit way to extend these choices over the strips while keeping the corner frames exact. Let \(P\) be the orthogonal projection onto the disk's normal bundle along \(C\). Projection of ordinary differentiation gives a connection \(P\,d\). On a corner patch with its full frame matrix \(e_C\), the connection

\begin{equation}
\nabla^C s
=P\,ds-(P\,de_C)(e_C^{\mathsf t}e_C)^{-1}e_C^{\mathsf t}s
\label{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.13}
\end{equation}

satisfies \(\nabla^C(e_C\xi)=e_C\,d\xi\). Thus \(e_C\) is parallel for this connection, without assuming it is orthonormal. Use smooth cutoff functions equal to one on smaller corner patches to add the displayed correction to \(P\,d\), and retain \(P\,d\) elsewhere. The corner patches are disjoint. This defines a smooth connection on the normal bundle of \(C\), equal to \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.13} wherever the strip and corner formulas overlap.

Transport the chosen boundary frame along each strip's \(t\)-lines using this connection. The resulting linear ordinary differential equations have invertible transport, so each transported triple remains a frame. They depend smoothly on \((s,t)\). Near the corners they reproduce exactly \(e_C\), by \eqref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:eq:7.13} and uniqueness. Thus the strips and quadrants give a single smooth positive frame \(e\) on the entire original annulus \(C\), adapted to both sheets on its outer boundary.

courseIII:creating-an-original-cancellin.text:
\subsection{An explicit global pair}
\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:pair-global-model}

\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-1}\label{courseIII:pair-creation:1}

On \(\mathbb R\times\mathbb R^k\times\mathbb R^{n-k-1}\), keep the coordinates \((u,y,z)\) and chosen positive coefficients

\begin{equation}
A(y)=\sum_{i=1}^k a_i y_i^2,\qquad
 B(z)=\sum_{j=1}^{n-k-1}b_j z_j^2.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

No coefficient is absorbed into a coordinate. Set

\begin{equation}
a=-2,\qquad
 \delta(u)=
 \begin{cases}4e^{-1/(1-u^2)},&|u|<1,\\0,&|u|\geq1,\end{cases}
 \qquad h(u)=u+\delta(u),\qquad
 F_b(u,y,z)=h(u)-A(y)+B(z).

\subsection{Cancellation, the complete derivative and the lower level}
\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:pair-cancelled-model}

\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-2}\label{courseIII:pair-creation:2}

Write \(v=A/R^2\). The full derivatives on the changed region are

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \partial_{y_i}F_c&=-2a_i y_i
       \left[1+\frac{\delta}{R^2}\beta'(v)\eta(B)\right],\\
 \partial_{z_j}F_c&=2b_j z_j
       \left[1-\delta\beta(v)\eta'(B)\right],\\
 \partial_uF_c&=h'-\delta'\beta(v)\eta(B)
       +2\delta\frac{R'}R\,v\beta'(v)\eta(B).
\end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

Here \(2RR'=h'\). In particular the variable-radius contribution is retained. The first bracket is at least \(1-(3/5)(4/3)=1/5\); the second is at least one. A critical point would have \(y=z=0\). But there \(F_c=u\) and \(\partial_uF_c=1\). Outside the changed region a possible critical point of \(F_b\) would be one of \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:1.5}, both of which lie in the changed region. Hence \(F_c\) has no critical point anywhere.

\subsection{A global product with its inverse}
\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:pair-global-product}

\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-3}\label{courseIII:pair-creation:3}

Use the Euclidean metric on the model coordinates solely to define the following auxiliary vector field:

\begin{equation}
V=\frac{\nabla F_c}{\|\nabla F_c\|^2}.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

There is a uniform positive lower bound for \(\|\nabla F_c\|\). Outside \(|u|\leq1\), the \(u\)-derivative is one. Inside that interval but outside a bounded transverse set, the modification is zero and the transverse gradient of \(F_b\), namely \((-2a_i y_i,2b_jz_j)\), has norm tending to infinity. The remaining set is compact, and the already proved absence of critical points gives a positive minimum there. Let \(m>0\) be the resulting bound. Then \(\|V\|\leq1/m\).

The field \(V\) is complete in both time directions. In finite time, its bounded speed keeps every trajectory in a compact Euclidean ball. On that ball it is smooth with bounded derivatives, and the elementary local ODE theorem extends a trajectory past any proposed finite endpoint. Moreover \(dF_c(V)=1\). Writing its global flow as \(\phi_s\), define

\begin{equation}
H(q_0,t)=\phi_{t-a}(j(q_0)),\qquad
 H:\mathbb R^{n-1}\times\mathbb R\longrightarrow\mathbb R^n.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:3.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{The exact descending field and its connecting trajectory}
\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:pair-connecting-trajectory}

\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-4}\label{courseIII:pair-creation:4}

Near either critical coordinate \(u_c\in\{u_M,u_m\}\), put

\begin{equation}
\alpha_c=\frac{|h''(u_c)|}{2},\qquad
 \sigma_M=-1,\quad\sigma_m=1,\qquad
 v_c(u)=(u-u_c)
 \sqrt{\frac{h(u)-h(u_c)}{\sigma_c\alpha_c(u-u_c)^2}}.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:4.1}
\end{equation}

The quotient extends smoothly with value one, by Taylor's integral formula. On a small interval it is positive and \(v_c'>0\), with \(v_c'(u_c)=1\). The exact equation is \(h=h(u_c)+\sigma_c\alpha_c v_c^2\).

Choose a smooth positive function \(\tau(u)\), equal to \(1/[2\alpha_c(v_c')^2]\) on smaller disjoint critical intervals and equal to one outside a compact subset of \((0,1)\). A convex interpolation by cutoffs gives such a function, bounded above and below by positive constants. Define

\begin{equation}
X_b=(-\tau(u)h'(u),\,y,\,-z).

\subsection{Insert the pair in the original band}
\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:pair-original-band}

\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-5}\label{courseIII:pair-creation:5}

Let \(f:C\to\mathbb R\) be the original Morse function and \(Z\) its descending field. Choose a regular product chart

\begin{equation}
\Gamma:Q\times J\longrightarrow C,\qquad f\Gamma(x,t)=t,
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

where \(Q\) is a coordinate ball in an original regular level and \(J\) is an open interval with no critical value. The \cref{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:trajectory-holonomy} constructs this chart from the original field and retains its full derivative. Choose its coordinate origin in \(Q\), and choose \(t_0\in J\).

The compact sets \(K'\), \(H^{-1}T\) and a neighbourhood of both critical points in \(H\)-coordinates fit in a finite coordinate rectangle. Enlarge that rectangle to contain all the values of \(F_b\) on \(T\), the value \(a\), and the two critical values in its height bound. Choose explicit positive scales \(\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_{n-1},\lambda\) small enough that

\begin{equation}
L_0(q_0,t)=(\epsilon_1q_{0,1},\ldots,\epsilon_{n-1}q_{0,n-1},
                         t_0+\lambda t)
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:5.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{The lower attaching sphere bounds its framed disk}
\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:pair-attaching-disk}

\label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section-6}\label{courseIII:pair-creation:6}

Write \(h_m=h(u_m)\). The lower unstable disk down to the original model \(a\)-level is exactly

\begin{equation}
D_q=\{(u_m,y,0):A(y)\leq h_m-a\}.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

It is embedded, its boundary lies in \(F_b^{-1}(a)\), and its interior is the lower unstable manifold truncated at that level. Apply \(H^{-1}\) to its boundary. Since \(H(q_0,a)=j(q_0)\), \eqref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:2.6} gives the exact lower attaching sphere in the original height product:

\begin{equation}
t=a,\quad z=0,\quad A(y_0)=u_m-a,\qquad
 y_0=\sqrt{\frac{u_m-a}{h_m-a}}\,y.
 \label{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:eq:6.2}
\end{equation}

In that very same level it bounds the disk

courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index.text:
\subsection{The original handle presentation gives the exact based group}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-original-group}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-1}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:1}

Let \((C;M_-,M_+)\) be the original connected smooth cobordism, of dimension \(n\geq6\), with nonempty connected boundary manifolds \(M_-,M_+\), simply connected incoming boundary \(M_-\), and simply connected \(C\). Use the included zero/top removal and index arrangement, retaining every original parameter radius, coefficient, gluing map and boundary comparison. Let \(W_1\) be the original stage consisting of the incoming collar and the \(r\) remaining index-one handles, and \(N_1\) its outgoing boundary. Write the retained one-handle coordinates as

\begin{equation}
[-r_i,r_i]\times D^{n-1}_{s_i},\qquad r_i,s_i>0.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

Their two attaching maps are the restrictions of the actual framed map to \(\{-r_i,r_i\}\times D^{n-1}_{s_i}\). Keep their labels and signs.

Choose a point \(b\) in the common exterior of all attaching disks, and paths \(a_i^\pm\) from \(b\) to the centres of the two feet. The path

\begin{equation}
h_i(t)=(r_i t,0),\quad -1\leq t\leq1,\qquad
 x_i=[a_i^-h_i(a_i^+)^{-1}]
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{The finite words that must be realized geometrically}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-normal-closure}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-2}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:2}

For any group \(G\) and labelled elements \(R_1,\ldots,R_m\), the normal closure of those elements is exactly the set of finite products

\begin{equation}
\prod_{\nu=1}^{N}g_\nu R_{j_\nu}^{\epsilon_\nu}g_\nu^{-1},
 \quad
 N\geq0,\quad g_\nu\in G,\quad
 \epsilon_\nu\in\{-1,1\}.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

Proof: the set of such products contains the identity and all the \(R_j\), is closed under multiplication by concatenating the lists, and is closed under inverse by reversing the list and replacing each sign by its negative. Conjugation by \(a\in G\) replaces every \(g_\nu\) by \(ag_\nu\). It is therefore a normal subgroup containing the given elements. Conversely every normal subgroup containing them contains each factor and every finite product. These two inclusions prove \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:2.1}.

Apply this to the exact group in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.9}. For every original one-handle label \(i\), there is a finite identity in the original free group

\begin{equation}
x_i=\prod_{\nu=1}^{N_i}

\subsection{A supported belt crossing gives the actual two-handle slide}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-belt-crossing}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-3}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:3}

We now construct the geometric operation directly in the original target-handle coordinates. Keep a target index-two handle \(j\), attached first to the original boundary \(N=N_1\). Write its radii as \(a_j,b_j>0\), its full framed attaching map as

\begin{equation}
\varphi_j:S^1_{a_j}\times D^{n-2}_{b_j}\longrightarrow N,
 \qquad L_j=\varphi_j(S^1_{a_j}\times\{0\}).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

Keep every other attaching map, with a moving label \(i\ne j\). Initially its whole image is disjoint from the target tube. It therefore lies in the common exterior before and after attaching \(j\).

Let \(S_j\) be the actual rounded outgoing boundary after this handle, and \(B_j\) its belt sphere. The already proved rounding and complement maps give the exact diffeomorphism

\begin{equation}
P_j:S_j\setminus B_j\longrightarrow N\setminus L_j.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:3.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{The exact based word and its crossing sign}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-based-sign}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-4}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:4}

Keep the source orientation of the moving circle and the ordered coordinates \((x_1,x_2)\) of the target core. For the positive crossing \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:3.9}, the before arc goes from negative to positive \(x_1\) below the origin, and the after arc goes in that same direction above it. The after arc followed by the inverse before arc winds clockwise around the origin. Its winding is therefore \(-1\), not \(+1\).

Here is a proof which retains the whole complement map. Remove a small open disk around \((v,t)=(0,1/2)\) from the parameter strip of \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:3.8}. On the remaining strip, \(P_j\) composed with the trace is defined. On a small positively oriented circle about the removed point, \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:3.9} gives degree \(+1\) for \(x/\|x\|\). Its image under \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:3.3} is

\begin{equation}
\varphi_j\left(a_j\frac{x}{\|x\|},
                   \frac{F_j(\|x\|)}{b_j}y_*\right).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:4.1}
\end{equation}

Within the actual original tube this loop is homotopic to the positively oriented attaching core by multiplying its second coordinate by \(1-s\), with \(0\leq s\leq1\). Its first coordinate retains degree \(+1\); the strict monotone radial function and every original radius remain in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:4.1}. The parameter strip boundary has the before arc forward and the after arc backward for orientation \(dv\wedge dt\). Its inner boundary has the negative orientation. Reading these three boundary pieces proves that the after arc followed by the inverse before arc contributes the negative target-core class. Reversing the crossing reverses this sign. This verifies the clockwise computation without a picture convention.

Let \(R_i^{\mathrm{old}}\) and \(R_i^{\mathrm{new}}\) be the exact based words, with the basepoint fixed away from all supports. Let \(h\) be the actual path from that basepoint to the target core basepoint, obtained by following the prefix of the moving circle to the crossing band and then its retained band and angular paths. Retain the target connecting path \(\omega_j\) from \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.8}, and set \(g=[h\omega_j^{-1}]\). Thus \(g\) is a based group element; the raw path to the target is not multiplied as though it were a based loop. Cutting the based loop at the changed arc and reading the punctured strip gives

\begin{equation}
R_i^{\mathrm{new}}

\subsection{From the exact loop class to a full ambient isotopy}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-loop-isotopy}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-5}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:5}

Put \(d=n-1\geq5\). We need the following relative statement on the actual \(d\)-dimensional level manifold \(N\). Let \(K\) be a finite disjoint union of embedded circles. Let

\begin{equation}
\lambda_0,\lambda_1:S^1_{a_0}\longrightarrow N\setminus K
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

be smooth parametrized embeddings which are freely homotopic in \(N\setminus K\). The original radius \(a_0>0\) is retained. There is a smooth isotopy \(\lambda_t\) through parametrized embeddings in this complement, constant on time intervals near \(0\) and \(1\), with these exact endpoints. If a given homotopy is fixed on an open parameter arc containing a specified closed arc, the construction can also fix a smaller open neighbourhood of that closed arc. In the application we do not need a fixed arc: the retained based words and connecting paths give the required free homotopy.

We prove the assertion, including the two different dimension estimates it requires. First make the given continuous homotopy smooth relative its time-end collars and any prescribed parameter-arc collar. Use the finite coordinate embedding, retraction and partition-of-unity smoothing constructed in \cref{courseIII:belt-sphere-complements-and-the-whitney-disk:section}, Sections 2--3. For completeness, on the compact image choose finitely many coordinate charts and chart cutoffs; the map formed from the cutoff-weighted coordinate functions and the cutoff functions embeds a neighbourhood of that compact image into a finite-dimensional Euclidean space. Its normal exponential map supplies a retraction from a neighbourhood. Approximate the Euclidean-valued homotopy by local convolution and a partition of unity, with a cutoff vanishing on the prescribed collars, then apply that retraction. A sufficiently close approximation remains in \(N\setminus K\). Convex interpolation before retraction proves that it is the same relative homotopy. Use the angular coordinate \(\theta\) only as a parameter on the original circle; its point is always \((a_0\cos\theta,a_0\sin\theta)\).

Here is the finite-parameter argument that removes vanishing tangents. Around a point of the parameter cylinder at which \(\partial_\theta h\) could vanish, choose a local source coordinate and a target chart. Perturb in that chart by

\begin{equation}
h_{u,v}(\theta,t)
   =h(\theta,t)

\subsection{The old two-handles and their exact descending comparison}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-original-flow}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:6}

The following preparation makes the use of the complement map precise. Start with the actual ordered Morse function and field supplied by \cref{courseIII:morse-trajectories-and-critical-value-lowering:trajectory-ordered-morse}, after zero/top removal. If there is no one-handle, no index-one cancellation is needed. Otherwise select the currently highest index-one critical point \(q\). Write its value as \(c_1\). Keep the full local coordinates and Hessian coefficients of this point.

\subsubsection{Putting the old two-handles at one critical value}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6.1}

There must be at least one old two-handle: if there were none, \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.9} would identify the nontrivial free group of the remaining one-handles with the trivial group of \(C\). Let \(c_2\) initially be the lowest old index-two critical value. We change the higher index-two critical values to this same \(c_2\), without changing any critical point, its index or its local Hessian.

Here is a finite induction which realizes the change. Suppose the first \(j\) old index-two points already have value \(c_2\), and take the next old index-two point \(p_j\), at value \(c_j>c_2\). Choose a regular level \(a\) with

\begin{equation}
c_1<a<c_2.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

The simultaneous outgoing boundary for the first \(j\) points has \(j\) disjoint belt spheres of codimension two. Its construction is given explicitly in the next paragraph; for \(j=1\) it is the included original Morse construction. At a regular level above \(c_2\) and below \(c_j\), their images under the actual outer flow are exactly the stable sections of these \(j\) points, by the proof of equation (1.12) in the trajectory companion, applied separately in their disjoint critical charts. Move the one-dimensional attaching circle of \(p_j\) off their union by the proved relative avoidance, since \(1<2\), and realize that whole isotopy as descending holonomy in a regular band above them. The compact-limit and hitting-time proof in that companion, \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-2}, now applies with this finite collection as the only possible critical limits. All have been excluded. It gives the complete embedded unstable disk of \(p_j\) down to \(a\). Apply the supported lowering formula in its \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-4} with new value \(c_2\); its strict inequality is
\(c_j-c_2<c_j-a\). Choose its neighbourhood disjoint from the other critical points and keep the original field on a collar of \(f=a\), where the function modification vanishes. This leaves the first \(j\) critical points unchanged and adds \(p_j\) at their value. Points of the same value cannot have a descending connecting trajectory, because the function strictly decreases on every nonconstant trajectory. This completes the induction after finitely many old two-handles.

Pull every complete attaching map of \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:6.2} back by \(J^{-1}\). This is a simultaneous presentation of all the old two-handles on \(N\); retain its original radii \(r_j,s_j\), its full maps \(\varphi_j\), and all derivatives \(DJ^{-1}D\varphi_j^{\,\mathrm{local}}\). This \(N\) is an actual level of the one-handle body. Its exact regular product to the earlier \(N_1\) carries all words and connecting paths of Sections 1--2. We write the transported based group as \(G\); no new free generators have silently replaced the old ones.

\subsubsection{The loop that actually crosses the selected one-handle once}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6.2}

Construct a specified embedded loop \(\kappa\) in \(N\) as follows. On the actual outgoing boundary of the highest one-handle \(q\), take its original longitudinal arc

\begin{equation}
u\longmapsto (u,v_*),\qquad
       -r_q\leq u\leq r_q,\quad v_*\in S^{n-2}_{s_q}.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:6.4}
\end{equation}

Join its two ends by an embedded exterior arc, with the needed endpoint germs. That exterior is connected: it is the preceding connected boundary with two disk interiors removed, and paths can be moved off the disk centres and then pushed radially out as in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.4}. Relative smoothing and the chart-parameter argument give an embedded connecting arc, disjoint from the longitudinal arc except at its designated ends. Round those ends in the retained attaching collars. The core of \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:6.4} crosses the original belt \(\{0\}\times S^{n-2}_{s_q}\) exactly once, with derivative \(\partial_u\) transverse to it. The rest of the loop misses that belt. Apply the actual rounding map, the outer flow map of the \(q\)-handle and the regular product to \(N\). Their invertible full derivatives preserve precisely that one transverse intersection. Denote the resulting stable section by

\begin{equation}
\Sigma_q=W^s(q)\cap N.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:6.5}
\end{equation}

There is no critical value between \(c_1\) and \(a\), so the equality follows from the stable-section proof in the trajectory companion and the actual regular product.

\subsection{Removing the selected index-one handle}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:slide-index-one-cancellation}

\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-7}\label{courseIII:low-index-slides:7}

We now apply all these constructions on the original cobordism. Keep the simultaneous old two-handle stage of Section 6. In a regular band above \(b\) and below the first old higher-index critical value, or below the outgoing collar if there is no such point, insert the supported two/three pair. Use a small coordinate disk in \(H(e(E))\) for its projection to the old level. The full compact support and the entire lower attaching disk of the pair fit in that coordinate product by the explicit positive scales of \cref{courseIII:creating-an-original-cancelling-handle-pair:section}, Section 5. Its \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-6} computes the disk and its full extended frame. Denote the new index-two point by \(p\) and its paired index-three point by \(r\). Their values satisfy

\begin{equation}
c_2<b<f(p)<f(r).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.1}
\end{equation}

Use a slightly smaller regular band below \(p\) to record the complete attaching tube pulled down to \(f=b\), and then through \(H^{-1}\). Call it

\begin{equation}
\alpha_0:S^1_{a_0}\times D^{n-2}_{b_0}\longrightarrow S.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.2}
\end{equation}

The choice of insertion disk puts its whole image in \(e(E)\), and its core bounds the specified disk there. Its word pulled to \(N\) is therefore the exact identity \(R_0=1\).

The final frame is the actual transported frame in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:5.14}. No extension of an arbitrary sphere diffeomorphism, and no assumed cancellation of a frame twist, was used.

\subsubsection{Realizing that exact tube as the unstable attachment}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-7.1}

Keep the function fixed for this stage. Transport the whole isotopy to the actual level \(f=b\):

\begin{equation}
\mathcal B_t=H\mathcal A_tH^{-1},\qquad
 D\mathcal B_t=DH\,D\mathcal A_t\,DH^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:7.6}
\end{equation}

Use a regular band \([b,b_1]\) below \(p\), with its actual old increasing product \(\Gamma\), and a smooth \(\eta\) equal to one near \(b\) and zero near \(b_1\). In those product coordinates the new descending field is the pushforward of \(-\partial_t\) by

\begin{equation}
F(z,t)=(\mathcal B_{\eta(t)}z,t),\qquad
 DF=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 D\mathcal B_{\eta(t)}&
       \eta'(t)\partial_s\mathcal B_s|_{s=\eta(t)}\\

This is the reduction now proved. The \cref{courseIII:integral-handle-chains-and-signed-incidences:handle-six-seven} now proves the original filtration-to-homology map, every incidence sign, the full integer basis comparisons and the exact contractions in dimensions six and seven. The \cref{courseIII:higher-index-framed-slides-and-chain-maps:higher-slide-matrices} now realizes every elementary integral change with its full framed cobordism map and proves both neighbouring differentials using the singular-prism correction. The \cref{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:receiving-whitney-flow} proves the full disk, adapted frame and supported move for the three required sheet-dimension pairs, with all original metric, orientation and framing maps retained. The \cref{courseIII:realizing-framed-handles-and-middle-cancellation:middle-six-seven-reduction} constructs the compatible function, proves the exact intersection-to-trajectory comparison and every escape and support hypothesis, and completes the simply connected smooth cobordism reductions in dimensions six and seven.

\subsubsection{A proved strengthening of the incoming hypothesis}
\label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-8.1}

The forward removal actually uses a weaker hypothesis than simple connectivity. Here is the exact statement and its proof. Let \(M_-\) be connected and suppose that its actual inclusion induces a surjection

\begin{equation}
\iota_*:\pi_1(M_-,b)\longrightarrow\pi_1(C,b).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:8.6}
\end{equation}

After zero/top removal, all index-one handles can still be removed by the construction above.

Put \(\Gamma=\pi_1(M_-,b)\), keeping its full multiplication and all its relations. Removing the finitely many attaching disks induces an isomorphism on this group: surjectivity moves loops off their centres and radially out; injectivity does the same to null-homotopies relative their boundary, exactly as in \eqref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:1.4}. The two-component path-class argument in \cref{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:section-1} therefore gives

\begin{equation}
\pi_1(W_1,b)=\Gamma*F(x_1,\ldots,x_r),\qquad
 \pi_1(N_1,b)\xrightarrow{\cong}\pi_1(W_1,b).
 \label{courseIII:framed-handle-slides-and-index-one-removal:eq:8.7}
\end{equation}

courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontr.text:
\begin{equation}
S_m(v,a,b)=(Qa,\ P_\nu a+Jv,\ b),\qquad
 S_m^2=I,\qquad
 \tau_E=\tau_{\mathrm{st}}S_m.
 \label{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:eq:8.11}
\end{equation}

Each equality follows by substituting these three matrix identities. Relative to the decomposition \(a=P_\nu a+JQa\), the map \(S_m\) exchanges the two \(n\)-dimensional tangent summands and fixes every other summand. Its determinant sign is therefore \((-1)^n\). In general an orientation calculation alone would not make this bundle automorphism homotopic to the identity.

For the original \(M=X\), retain its original almost-complex tangent operator \(\mathcal J\), with \(\mathcal J^2=-I\). It is provided by the original complex charts, independently of the later smooth-sphere recognition. This gives the following explicit homotopy, which proves the additional comparison we actually need. For \(0\leq t\leq1\), put \(\theta=\pi t/2\), \(\varphi=\pi t\), \(u=Qa\), \(y=P_\nu a\), and set

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 C_\varphi&=\cos\varphi\,I+\sin\varphi\,\mathcal J,\qquad
       C_\varphi^{-1}=\cos\varphi\,I-\sin\varphi\,\mathcal J,\\
 v_t&=\cos\theta\,v+\sin\theta\,u,\\
 a_t&=y+J C_\varphi(-\sin\theta\,v+\cos\theta\,u),\\
 S_{m,t}(v,a,b)&=(v_t,a_t,b).
 \end{aligned}
 \label{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:eq:8.12}
\end{equation}

\label{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:pt-full-tube-and-collapse}

\subsection{The original-radius tube and its smooth collapse}
\label{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:section-3}

\label{courseIII:pontryagin-thom:3}

For a positive number \(\epsilon\), retain the actual ellipsoidal fiber condition

\begin{equation}
\mathcal U_\epsilon=
 \{(m,A)\in M\times\mathbb R^r:
                 \rho(m,A)=A^{\mathsf T}G(m)A<\epsilon^2\}.
 \label{courseIII:framed-manifolds-and-the-pontryagin-thom-map:eq:3.1}
