courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original.text:
\subsection{A smooth twist in the actual three-sphere tube}
\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-actual-tube}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-1}

Let \(P\) be a closed oriented smooth six-manifold, and let

\begin{equation}
f:S^3\longrightarrow P
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

be an oriented parametrized embedding. The sphere is the unit sphere in the original ordered quaternion space \(\mathbb H=(1,i,j,k)\), with its boundary orientation. Its oriented rank-three normal bundle is trivial: its hemisphere transition has class in \(\pi_2SO(3)=0\), as proved in the geometric framing chapter, Sections 1--2. Retain a chosen normal frame \(\psi_q:\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H\to\nu_{f,q}\), an actual tubular map and a positive coordinate radius \(R\) on which that map is injective.

The left quaternion frame gives the exact bundle isomorphisms

\begin{equation}
TS^3\longleftrightarrow S^3\times\operatorname{Im}\mathbb H,
 \qquad(q,z)\longmapsto(q,\bar qz),\qquad
 (q,h)\longmapsto(q,qh).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.2}

\subsection{The full signed integral action}
\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-signed-action}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-2}

Write \(v=f_*[S^3]\in H_3(P;\mathbb Z)\), and write \(\lambda(x,y)=x\cdot y\) for the ordered intersection number using the original orientation. The normal orientation in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:1.3} is the one for which an oriented tangent frame of \(f(S^3)\), followed by its normal frame, is positive in \(P\).

We prove

\begin{equation}
(\mathcal F_f)_*(x)=x-\lambda(v,x)v,
       \qquad
       (\mathcal T_f)_*(x)=x+\lambda(v,x)v,
       \qquad \mathcal T_f=\mathcal F_f^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

This includes the integer sign, rather than only its residue modulo two.

First define the variation map on the original tube:

\subsection{Integral Lagrangians and a finite sequence of the actual twists}
\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-integral-lagrangians}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-3}

Let \(H\) be a free integral group of rank \(2r\), with a unimodular alternating pairing \(\lambda\). A primitive subgroup \(L\) means that \(H/L\) is torsion-free. An integral Lagrangian is a primitive rank-\(r\) subgroup on which \(\lambda\) vanishes. We prove that a finite product of the transvections \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:2.6} carries any such \(L\) to any specified integral Lagrangian. All changes below are actual automorphisms of \(H\); the original group and pairing remain fixed.

First construct a symplectic basis adapted to an original Lagrangian \(B\). Choose its basis \(e_1,\ldots,e_r\). Primitivity and the subgroup theorem over \(\mathbb Z\) split \(H=B\oplus B'\). Hence every functional on \(B\) extends to \(H\). Unimodularity then supplies \(y_j\in H\) with

\begin{equation}
\lambda(e_i,y_j)=\delta_{ij}.
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

Write \(C_{jk}=\lambda(y_j,y_k)\), retaining the full skew integer matrix. Define coefficients \(a_{jk}=C_{jk}\) for \(j<k\), and \(a_{jk}=0\) for \(j\ge k\), and put

\begin{equation}
f_j=y_j+\sum_i a_{ij}e_i .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:3.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{The two integral Lagrangians in the original middle level}
\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-middle-level}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-4}

Let \(\Sigma\) be the original oriented smooth homotopy six-sphere. Retain its specified stable tangent framing. Section 9 of the \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:framed-six-original-nullbordism} constructs a compact framed seven-manifold \(W\), together with the actual boundary identification

\begin{equation}
\iota_\Sigma:\Sigma\xrightarrow{\cong}\partial W .
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.1}
\end{equation}

This is the framed nullbordism supplied by the proved geometric invariant and original integral stable-group calculation. It does not presuppose a smooth identification of \(\Sigma\) with a standard sphere.

Choose an oriented coordinate embedding \(\chi:D^7_\rho\to\operatorname{int}W\), with its actual radius \(\rho>0\), and remove the open coordinate ball. The incoming parametrization is

\begin{equation}
j:S^6\longrightarrow\partial\chi(D^7_\rho),\qquad
                 j(q)=\chi(\rho q),\qquad Dj_q(v)=D\chi_{\rho q}(\rho v).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Regluing the original pieces and retaining the frame defect}
\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-gluing}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-5}

Write \(i_-:P\to\partial_+C_-\) and \(i_+:P\to\partial_-C_+\) for the inclusions in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.3}. Their original gluing identifies \(i_+(p)\) with \(i_-(p)\). Define a new cobordism by the actual equivalence relation

\begin{equation}
C_\phi=(C_-\sqcup C_+)/\bigl(i_+(p)\sim i_-(\phi(p))\bigr).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

The two outer boundary maps \(j\) and \(\iota_\Sigma\) are retained literally. The natural maps of each piece into \(C'\) and \(C_\phi\) identify the interiors and their outer collars. They do not claim an identification of the two entire fillings; their old and new middle homology may differ.

Here is the smooth seam. Retain original collar parameters

\begin{equation}
c_-:P\times(-d_-,0]\to C_-,\qquad
 c_+:P\times[0,d_+)\to C_+,
 \qquad d_-,d_+>0,
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.2}

\subsection{The new signed matrix and cancellation of every actual handle}
\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-actual-cancellation}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-6}

Define \(F_i=\sigma_i f_i\). The classes \(F_i\) are still a basis of the complementary Lagrangian. Since \(\phi_*\) maps the basis \(a_j\) of \(L\) onto another basis of this same subgroup, there is a unique unimodular integer matrix \(U\) with

\begin{equation}
\phi_*a_j=\sum_iF_iU_{ij},\qquad
        U_{ij}=\sigma_i\lambda(b_i,\phi_*a_j).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

The equality on the right follows from \(\lambda(b_i,f_k)=\delta_{ik}\) and \(\sigma_i^2=1\). Its determinant is \(1\) or \(-1\) because both displayed collections are bases of the same free group.

The full relative handle chain complex of \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:5.1}, in its original lower and transported upper core bases, is

\begin{equation}
0\longrightarrow\mathbb Z^r
                \xrightarrow{\,D^\phi=U\,}\mathbb Z^r
                       \longrightarrow0 .

\subsection{The smooth map to the original sphere and its original homotopy class}
\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:twist-original-smooth-map}

\label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:section-7}

Restrict the product map to its outgoing boundary and use the original boundary identification:

\begin{equation}
d:S^6\longrightarrow\Sigma,\qquad
              d(q)=\iota_\Sigma^{-1}\bigl(\Psi(q,T)\bigr).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.1}
\end{equation}

Every factor in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.1} is a specified smooth map and \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:6.8} gives its smooth inverse:

\begin{equation}
d^{-1}(x)=j^{-1}\operatorname{Fl}^{Y}_{-T}(\iota_\Sigma(x)).
 \label{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:7.2}
\end{equation}

It is orientation-preserving. The positive orientation on the incoming level was specified by the disc in \eqref{courseIII:sphere-twists-and-the-original-smooth-six-sphere-comparison:eq:4.2}. The directed product orientation has boundary \(-S^6\) at \(0\) and \(+\Sigma\) at \(T\). The flow preserves the level orientation since its transverse time column stays positive. All intervening comparisons are orientation-preserving. Hence \(d_*[S^6]=[\Sigma]\), with the original orientations, without an unrecorded reflection.

courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-compa.text:
\subsection{The actual frame fibration kills the stabilized class}
\label{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:framing-so7}

\label{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:section-5}\label{courseIII:spin6:5}

Take the first-column map

\begin{equation}
SO(7)\longrightarrow S^6,
\qquad A\longmapsto Ae_1.
\label{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:eq:5.1}
\end{equation}

Its fibre over \(e_1\) is \(\operatorname{diag}(1,SO(6))\). Projection and Gram--Schmidt, followed by the orientation choice, give local sections, exactly as for the unitary fibration in Section 2. The associated rank-six bundle is the actual outward-oriented tangent bundle:

\begin{equation}
[A,v]\longmapsto
\bigl(Ae_1;A(0,v)\bigr)\in TS^6.
\label{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:eq:5.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Apply the comparison to the original homotopy sphere}
\label{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:framing-original-sphere}

\label{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:section-6}\label{courseIII:spin6:6}

Let \(g:S^6\to\Sigma\) be the positive-degree homotopy equivalence constructed in lesson 7, and let \(h:\Sigma\to S^6\) be its homotopy inverse. The bundle

\begin{equation}
g^*(T\Sigma\oplus\mathbf1_{\mathbb R})
\label{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:eq:6.1}
\end{equation}

is an oriented rank-seven bundle over \(S^6\), so \cref{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:section-5} gives a trivialization. Pull it back along \(h\). The homotopy \(g\circ h\simeq\operatorname{id}_\Sigma\) and the finite close-projection construction of lesson 11 identify the resulting pullback bundle with the actual \(T\Sigma\oplus\mathbf1_{\mathbb R}\). Thus it is continuously trivial.

It is smoothly trivial as well. In finitely many local smooth bundle frames, approximate the components of its seven continuous global frame sections uniformly by smooth functions, and combine them with a smooth partition of unity. Compactness gives a positive lower bound on the least singular value of the original frame map. An approximation closer than that bound preserves pointwise linear independence and the orientation sign. The resulting smooth sections are a smooth frame. If desired, Gram--Schmidt makes it orthonormal without changing its orientation. Denote the resulting frame isomorphism by

\begin{equation}
\mathcal F:\Sigma\times\mathbb R^7
\xrightarrow{\ \simeq\ }T\Sigma\oplus\mathbf1_{\mathbb R}.
\label{courseIII:spin-six-and-the-framing-comparison:eq:6.2}
\end{equation}

courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-f.text:
\begin{equation}
\pi_3(SO(3))\cong\pi_3(S^3)\cong\mathbb Z,\qquad
 \pi_2(SO(3))=0.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.3}
\end{equation}

The generator in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.3} is the original conjugation map on the positively oriented \(S^3\), not an unspecified choice.

The corresponding four-dimensional map is

\begin{equation}
\rho_4:S^3\times S^3\longrightarrow SO(\mathbb H),
 \qquad \rho_4(a,b)(v)=av\bar b.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.4}
\end{equation}

It preserves the full norm \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.1} and orientation. Its kernel consists of \((1,1)\) and \((-1,-1)\): apply the identity action to \(v=1\), then to every imaginary basis element. Its derivative sends \((\alpha,\beta)\) to \(v\mapsto\alpha v-v\beta\); vanishing first gives \(\alpha=\beta\), then makes this imaginary quaternion commute with every quaternion, hence zero. The derivative is an isomorphism onto \(\mathfrak{so}(4)\) by dimension. Its compact image is both closed and open in the connected \(SO(4)\), so \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.4} is onto and is the indicated two-sheeted cover. Connectedness of \(SO(4)\) follows directly by successively rotating its oriented orthonormal columns to the coordinate columns in coordinate two-planes. The stabilizer of the first vector \(1\in\mathbb H\) has \(a=b\). Consequently the original inclusion fixing that first coordinate is exactly

\begin{equation}
SO(3)\longrightarrow SO(4),\qquad
 [a]\longmapsto\rho_4(a,a).

\subsection{The full rank-three framing map and its factor two}
\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:framed-six-rank-three}

\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-1}\label{courseIII:framed6:1}

Use the original quaternion coordinates

\begin{equation}
q=q_0+q_1i+q_2j+q_3k,\qquad
 i^2=j^2=k^2=-1,\quad ij=k,\quad ji=-k,
 \quad |q|^2=q_0^2+q_1^2+q_2^2+q_3^2.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

The other two cyclic products are \(jk=i\) and \(ki=j\), and reversing either order changes its sign. Quaternion conjugation changes all three imaginary signs. Thus \(q\bar q=\bar q q=|q|^2\). Let \(S^3\) be the original unit sphere in these four ordered coordinates, with its positive boundary orientation.

For a unit quaternion \(a\), conjugation \(v\mapsto av\bar a\) preserves the imaginary subspace with coordinates \((i,j,k)\) and its Euclidean norm. It lies in \(SO(3)\), since \(S^3\) is connected and at \(a=1\) it is the identity. Its kernel is \(\{1,-1\}\): a quaternion commuting with \(i,j,k\) is real. The map is onto. Indeed for a unit imaginary quaternion \(u\), write \(a=\cos t+u\sin t\). It fixes \(u\); for an imaginary \(v\perp u\), direct multiplication using \(uv=-vu\) gives

\begin{equation}
a v\bar a=\cos(2t)v+\sin(2t)uv.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.2}

\subsection{The obstruction for an actual embedded three-sphere}
\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:framed-six-embedded-obstruction}

\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-2}\label{courseIII:framed6:2}

Let \(M\) be an oriented smooth six-manifold, and retain a specified stable tangent framing

\begin{equation}
\tau:TM\oplus\underline{\mathbb R}^{\,s}
       \longrightarrow\underline{\mathbb R}^{\,6+s}.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:2.1}
\end{equation}

Here a framing is the displayed fibrewise linear isomorphism, in the displayed order. Fix an orientation-preserving parametrized embedding \(f:S^3\hookrightarrow M\), where the sphere has the quaternion coordinates \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.1}. Its normal bundle has rank three. Give it the orientation for which the ordered sum \(TS^3\oplus\nu_f\) has the pullback orientation of \(TM\).

This oriented normal bundle is trivial. To prove this without replacing it by its stable class, choose trivializations over the two hemispheres of \(S^3\). Their transition on the equatorial \(S^2\) is a map to \(GL^+(3,\mathbb R)\), and the positive polar deformation retracts that group to \(SO(3)\). Equation \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:1.3} says the transition is nullhomotopic. Extending that nullhomotopy over one hemisphere changes its trivialization so that the two agree on the equator. The resulting three sections trivialize the original normal bundle. Denote an oriented normal frame by

\begin{equation}
\phi_q:\mathbb R^3\longrightarrow(\nu_f)_q.
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:2.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Framed bordism invariance}
\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:framed-six-bordism-invariance}

\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-8}\label{courseIII:framed6:8}

We first prove the change across an actual stage of index-three handles, then reduce an arbitrary framed bordism between two-connected six-manifolds to two such stages. The reduction needs vanishing in relative degree two, not an assumption that the whole bordism is a homology equivalence.

\subsubsection{A complete index-three stage}
\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-8.1}

Let \(T\) be the trace obtained from \(M\times[0,1]\), with \(M\) as in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-6}, by attaching \(r\) handles \(D^3_{a_j}\times D^4_{b_j}\) of index three. Retain every radius \(a_j,b_j>0\), attaching map and original orientation. Suppose \(T\) has its specified stable framing extending that on \(M\). Write its outgoing boundary as \(P\) with its induced frame. The sphere-attaching maps have domain \(S^2_{a_j}\). Since \(M\) is two-connected they are nullhomotopic.

The pair \((T,M)\) has a relative CW model with precisely \(r\) cells of dimension three, and the reversed pair \((T,P)\) has precisely \(r\) cells of dimension four. These are the original core and cocore models from the handle-chain companion. The first description gives \(\pi_1T=\pi_2T=0\); the second shows that \(P\to T\) induces isomorphisms on \(\pi_1,\pi_2\). In the latter claim the relative groups through degree three vanish because all relative cells have dimension four. Thus \(P\) is also two-connected, and \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-6} applies to it.

Put \(H=H_3(M;\mathbb Z)\), \(K=H_3(T;\mathbb Z)\), and \(V=H_3(P;\mathbb Z)\). The complete relevant integral sequences are

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 0&\longrightarrow H\xrightarrow{\,j\,}K
               \xrightarrow{\,r_3\,}\mathbb Z^r\longrightarrow0,\\
 0&\longrightarrow\mathbb Z^r\xrightarrow{\,\delta\,}V

This proves invariance across the entire original index-three stage.

\subsubsection{Making the interior two-connected with the stable frame retained}
\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-8.2}

Let \(C^7\) be a compact framed bordism from two-connected closed \(M^6\) to two-connected closed \(N^6\). We may make its interior connected without changing its boundary. In the eight-dimensional product trace, attach an index-one handle along two seven-discs in distinct components of the upper interior face, away from the original boundary. Its new upper face is obtained by removing those two seven-balls and joining their six-spheres by a tube. The frame on each small attaching disc is homotopic to its centre value by contraction, with that homotopy implemented in its collar. The two centre frames join by a path in the appropriate connected component of invertible matrices; the orientations of the two attaching feet fix that component. Extend over the product one-handle, then restore the full prescribed disc frames using those collar homotopies. This gives the new upper face its stable frame and leaves both original boundary framings fixed.

Next make \(\pi_1C\) and \(\pi_2C\) zero by framed surgeries in the interior. Here are the exact existence and extension arguments. A finite handle CW model gives finitely many generators of \(\pi_1C\). Represent them by disjoint embedded loops: the coincidence and avoidance dimensions for finitely many curves are at most two, less than seven. Their actual oriented normal bundles have rank six and are trivial because an oriented bundle on \(S^1\) is obtained by identifying its end frames by an element of the connected \(SO(6)\).

For a surgery on an embedded \(S^k\subset C^7\), \(k=1,2\), compare the original stable frame on the attaching region with the parameter frame on \(D^{k+1}\times D^{7-k}\). The comparison is the same full radial splitting as \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:2.3}--\eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:2.4}, with \(z\in\mathbb R^{k+1}\), radial component \(\langle q,z\rangle\), and last coordinate \(-\langle q,z\rangle\). Its extension obstruction is in \(\pi_kSO(8+s)\). Changing the actual normal frame changes it by the inclusion from \(\pi_kSO(7-k)\), by the product calculation of \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:2.8}. This is the same extension-over-the-core argument used in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-2}, with the original dimensions displayed.

For \(k=1\), the map

\begin{equation}
\pi_1SO(6)\longrightarrow\pi_1SO(8+s)
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.8}
\end{equation}

is an isomorphism. Each successive sphere bundle \(SO(j)\to SO(j+1)\to S^j\), \(j\ge6\), has zero adjacent sphere groups in degrees one and two, so its exact sequence proves this statement. Choose the normal-frame class which cancels the obstruction. The resulting framed surgery trace attaches an index-two handle in dimension eight; seen from the outgoing seven-manifold its dual handle has index six. Attaching the original two-handles kills the normal closure of the chosen loop generators in \(\pi_1\), and the outgoing inclusion preserves \(\pi_1\), since its relative cells have dimension six. Thus the new interior is simply connected.

Its \(\pi_2\) is finitely generated: Hurewicz identifies it with the finitely generated \(H_2\) of the compact manifold. Represent a finite set of generators by disjoint embedded two-spheres in the interior. The derivative rank-loss codimension exceeds two, the self-coincidence dimension is four, and \(4<7\); the same relative chart perturbation gives the embeddings and mutual disjointness.

Attach the resulting framed index-three traces in dimension eight. Their outgoing dual handles have index five. The incoming cell attachment quotients \(\pi_2\) by the selected generators, as follows from the exact homotopy sequence of the relative three-cell attachment on a simply connected space. The outgoing inclusion is an isomorphism on \(\pi_1,\pi_2\), since its relative cells have dimension five. Thus the final \(C'\) is two-connected. All surgeries and their complete frame extensions took place in the interior, so the original boundary manifolds, boundary maps and stable frames are retained. Extra stable outward-normal coordinates produced by the traces are retained as appended coordinates; the stabilization invariance proved in \cref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-2} compares them with the original boundary frames.

\subsubsection{Only indices three and four are needed}
\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-8.3}

Take a finite relative Morse presentation of \(C'\), with its actual incoming and outgoing collars. The included zero/top removal applies because \(C'\) and both boundaries are connected. The included index-one removal applies because \(C'\) and both boundaries are simply connected; its proof explicitly assumes only those fundamental-group conditions. Apply it also to the reversed cobordism. This gives precisely the index range \(2,3,4,5\).

The long exact sequences of the two original pairs give

\begin{equation}
H_2(C',M;\mathbb Z)=0,\qquad
                 H_2(C',N;\mathbb Z)=0,
 \label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.10}
\end{equation}

since \(H_2(C')=0\) and \(H_1(M)=H_1(N)=0\). After index-one removal the relative degree-two chain group has zero outgoing differential. The first equality in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.10} therefore says that its degree-three boundary is onto. The included integral handle-chain proof constructs an integer section and an actual kernel basis, making that boundary \((I\ \ 0)\) by a recorded invertible upper-basis change. The higher-handle-slide companion realizes this change by the full original framed slides.

Use the included Whitney construction with sheet dimensions \((2,4)\) in the six-dimensional regular level. The lower level is the original simply connected \(M\). It removes opposite-sign intersection pairs and turns the displayed incidence coefficients into exactly one or zero geometric intersections. Sections 2--5 of \emph{Actual Morse data for the modified framed handles} realize that presentation by actual Morse data, identify every intersection with its unique trajectory, prove escape and compact support, and cancel all the index-two handles with their selected index-three partners.

Only indices \(3,4,5\) remain. Reverse the original cobordism, retaining the reversal maps and every frame. Its indices are \(2,3,4\), and the second equality in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.10} supplies the same surjectivity in relative degree two. The identical argument cancels the reversed index-two handles with reversed index-three handles. In the original direction these are indices five and four. No lower index is introduced. Consequently the final presentation of the same \(C'\), with both original boundary maps retained, has only

\subsection{The original framed homotopy six-sphere has a framed nullbordism}
\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:framed-six-original-nullbordism}

\label{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:section-9}\label{courseIII:framed6:9}

The included Pontryagin--Thom companion proves the actual map and both inverse bordism/homotopy constructions

\begin{equation}
