The product with the actual Lie framing \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:3.6} has \(\kappa=1\) by \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:6.24} and \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.13}. Thus \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:8.13} is a nonzero homomorphism from the two-element group in \eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.1}--\eqref{courseIII:the-original-six-dimensional-framing-invariant:eq:9.2} to \(\mathbb F_2\). It is consequently an isomorphism: its two elements have distinct images. In particular

courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-s.text:
\subsection{A full flow in the specified model}
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:move-model-flow}

\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:section-1}\label{courseIII:whitney-support:1}

Retain positive parameters \(R,d,b\). In coordinates
\((x,y,z_1,z_2,z_3)\in\mathbb R^5\), put

\begin{equation}
\phi(x)=R^2-x^2,\qquad
D_R=\{(x,y,0,0,0):-R\leq x\leq R,\ 0\leq y\leq\phi(x)\}.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.1}
\end{equation}

The two sheet models, on a neighbourhood of this disk, are

\begin{equation}
A_0=\{(x,\phi(x),z_1,0,0)\},\qquad
B_0=\{(x,0,0,z_2,z_3)\}.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.2}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Every cutoff derivative in the ambient inverse}
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:move-full-derivative}

\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:section-2}\label{courseIII:whitney-support:2}

The full derivatives of the functions in \eqref{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:1.3} are

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\phi'(x)&=-2x,\\
\delta'(x)&=-2x\left[
 \chi(u(x))+\frac{4(\phi(x)+d)}d\chi'(u(x))\right],\\
\eta_x(x,y)&=-\frac{2x}{d}\chi(L(y))\chi'(H(x,y)),\\
\eta_y(x,y)&=\frac1d\left[
 \chi'(L(y))\chi(H(x,y))-\chi(L(y))\chi'(H(x,y))\right],\\
\zeta_{z_j}(z)&=-\frac{4z_j}{b^2}\chi'(Z(z)),\\
v_x&=\delta'\eta\zeta+\delta\eta_x\zeta,\qquad
v_y=\delta\eta_y\zeta,\qquad
v_{z_j}=\delta\eta\zeta_{z_j}.
\end{aligned}
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:2.1}

\subsection{Extending an entire boundary collar map}
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:move-collar-extension}

\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:section-3}\label{courseIII:whitney-support:3}

We first prove the coordinate extension that the original disk needs. The radius \(h>0\) in this section is the radius of its original round filling disk. A collar germ means a map defined on some whole open band about the boundary; two such maps define the same germ if they agree on a smaller whole band.

\textbf{Collar extension.} A smooth diffeomorphism germ from a collar of \(\partial D_h^2\) to itself, taking the inward side to the inward side, extends to a diffeomorphism of \(D_h^2\) that agrees with that entire germ on a smaller collar.

First suppose the boundary map preserves orientation. Write its angular lift as \(f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R\), with

\begin{equation}
f(\theta+2\pi)=f(\theta)+2\pi,\qquad f'(\theta)>0.
\label{courseIII:whitney-move-with-controlled-support:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

Choose a smooth function \(a_0:[0,h]\to[0,1]\), zero near \(0\) and one near \(h\). The maps

\begin{equation}
F_s(r,\theta)=
\bigl(r,\ \theta+s\,a_0(r)(f(\theta)-\theta)\bigr),

