\subsection{Rank-four frames and the exact rotation obstruction}
\label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:receiving-rank-four-frame}

\label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:section-3}\label{courseIII:whitney-receiving:3}

Use the real quaternions \(\mathbb H\) with their original ordered basis \((1,i,j,k)\), multiplication \(ij=k,jk=i,ki=j\), reversed products with minus signs, and \(i^2=j^2=k^2=-1\). Their associative matrix realization, conjugation and full norm identity are proved in \cref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section}, Section 2. No alteration of the receiving disk coordinates is made by this auxiliary algebra.

For unit quaternions define

\begin{equation}
\rho:S^3\times S^3\longrightarrow SO(4),\qquad
 \rho(a,b)(x)=a x b^{-1}.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.1}
\end{equation}

Norm multiplicativity proves orthogonality. Its determinant is \(+1\), since the domain is connected and the identity pair acts as the identity. It is a homomorphism: the two left multipliers multiply in order, and the right multipliers do too after taking the inverse. If it acts trivially, substitution \(x=1\) gives \(a=b\), and then \(a\) commutes with \(i,j,k\). Comparing its four coefficients gives zero imaginary part. Thus

\begin{equation}
\ker\rho=\{(1,1),(-1,-1)\}.
 \label{courseIII:whitney-moves-in-the-receiving-dimensions:eq:3.2}
\end{equation}

